    "created_at": "2016-02-01T16:06:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-01T16:06:58Z",
    "body": "I've changed the title to broaden the scope. In https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/509#issuecomment-174967812 and https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/509#issuecomment-177914176 I complain about how these errors work, and this issue is about making sense of it.\n- After reaching `networkState` `HAVE_CURRENT_DATA` or greater, are there actually any _fatal_ errors in implementations, errors that make it impossible for the media element to ever play again without starting over with `load()`? As written, there's nothing to prevent seeking or playing after the errors are reported.\n- For non-fatal network or decode errors, how should they be reported? There could be any number of such errors during the lifetime of a media element, but we have only a single `error` attribute which is only assigned once.\n- Can network and decode errors change `duration`, `buffered` and `seekable`? The spec doesn't say so, but it would make sense for a file found to only contain garbage after time _x_ to clamp the timeline to _x_.\n\nMy rough idea is to limit the `error` attribute to truly fatal errors, so that attempting to do anything with the media element while the `error` is a no-op, or could throw/reject with `InvalidStateError`. I suspect that only `MEDIA_ERR_SRC_NOT_SUPPORTED` would be included in this group. For non-fatal errors, we could add a way to report them with better detail, at least for same-origin data.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
